// Relativization / partition-safety, `previous` operator.
//
// A Read is permitted iff A's IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING OWN event (within 1h) was
// a Login. The event schema pins `callerPrincipal` universally, so `previous`
// means "this principal's previous event", not "the previous event in the
// global trace".
//
// The two traces are a differential pair for principal alice's Read at @3:
//   trace_1: alice Login @1, BOB Transfer @2 (foreign), alice Read @3.
//   trace_2: alice Login @1,                             alice Read @3.
// Under key-local semantics bob's interleaved Transfer is invisible to alice,
// so alice's previous own event is the Login in BOTH traces → PERMIT in both.
// Under (incorrect) global semantics, trace_1's previous event would be bob's
// Transfer → deny, diverging from trace_2. The rewrite makes them agree.
//
// alice's Read is the only decision event; bob has no decision. The verdict
// for alice's Read must be `true` in both traces (only its trace-position
// index differs).

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    previous within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
